    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">01 / Definition</p>
    <h2>What is AI search optimisation?</h2>

    <p>AI search optimisation is the process of making your website, content, and entity data discoverable and citable by the artificial intelligence systems that increasingly answer user queries. It is distinct from traditional SEO in ways that run deeper than most businesses realise.</p>

    <p>Traditional SEO is positional: rank higher, earn the click. AI search optimisation is citational: when a tool writes an answer, your business should be one of the sources it names.</p>

    <p>This matters because some research now starts in AI tools. A business owner might ask Copilot for software options. A homebuyer might ask ChatGPT what to look for in a survey. In each case the tool writes one answer and may name a few sources. AI search optimisation is the work of being one of them, named correctly.</p>

    <h3>The three pillars</h3>

    <p>AI search optimisation rests on three overlapping disciplines, each with its own logic but sharing a common foundation:</p>

    <dl class="def-grid not-prose">
  <div><dt>AEO</dt><dd>Answer Engine Optimisation targets direct-answer formats in Google AI Overviews, featured snippets, and voice assistants.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>GEO</dt><dd>Generative Engine Optimisation focuses on how generative AI platforms select and cite sources.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>LLM SEO</dt><dd>Addresses how large language models index, retrieve, and surface information from your site.</dd></div>
</dl>

    <p>They share one base: clear structure, one consistent set of business facts, and pages crawlers can reach.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">02 / Landscape</p>
    <h2>The AI search landscape in 2026</h2>

    <p>Each platform retrieves and cites differently. The way each one picks sources decides what to do.</p>

    <h3>ChatGPT and SearchGPT</h3>

    <p>OpenAI's ChatGPT answers from its training data and, when it searches, from live pages fetched by OAI-SearchBot. Cited pages are linked in the answer.</p>

    <p>ChatGPT is particularly relevant to Guildford businesses for research-stage queries. A potential client at <strong>Surrey Research Park</strong> evaluating software vendors, for example, might ask ChatGPT to compare options rather than running a traditional Google search.</p>

    <h3>Google AI Overviews</h3>

    <p>Google AI Overviews appear above the organic results for some queries, summarise several sources and link to them. They draw on Google's normal index. A page has to be indexed and eligible for Search.</p>

    <p>AI Overviews use Google's own index as their source material. This means traditional SEO and AI search optimisation are closely linked on this platform. Pages that rank well organically are more likely to be cited in AI Overviews. The selection criteria also favour content that directly answers the query, uses structured data and demonstrates topical authority.</p>

    <h3>Perplexity</h3>

    <p>Perplexity operates as a dedicated AI search engine. Every answer includes inline citations with numbered references linking to source pages. Perplexity uses real-time web search combined with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). It sends queries to multiple search engines and retrieves and ranks results. It then uses an LLM to synthesise an answer.</p>

    <p>Perplexity's citation-heavy format makes it one of the most transparent AI search platforms. Optimising for Perplexity shares significant overlap with traditional SEO: pages that rank well in organic search and have clear, well-structured content are more likely to be retrieved and cited.</p>

    <ChecklistFigure title="AI platforms that matter" items={["ChatGPT and SearchGPT","Google AI Overviews","Perplexity","Claude","Microsoft Copilot"]} caption="Each platform retrieves and cites sources in its own way." />

    <h3>Claude</h3>

    <p>Anthropic's Claude is used extensively in professional and enterprise contexts. Claude does not browse the web in all configurations. It has access to web search in some deployments. Its training data includes a broad corpus of web content. Businesses with strong entity presence and authoritative content are more likely to be referenced in Claude's responses.</p>

    <h3>Microsoft Copilot</h3>

    <p>Microsoft Copilot is integrated into Bing, Edge, Windows and the Microsoft 365 suite. Copilot is significant for B2B businesses in Guildford because of its integration into the tools that knowledge workers use daily. When an employee at a <strong>Surrey Research Park</strong> company asks Copilot a question within Microsoft Teams or Edge, the system retrieves information from Bing's index and synthesises an answer with citations.</p>

    <p>Copilot's reliance on Bing's index means that Bing SEO, often neglected by UK businesses, becomes directly relevant to AI search visibility. Submitting sitemaps to Bing Webmaster Tools and ensuring Bing can crawl your site are foundational steps.</p>

