    <div class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">03 / AEO</p>
    <h2>AEO: answer engine optimisation</h2>

    <p>Answer Engine Optimisation is the practice of structuring content so that AI-powered answer engines select it as the source for direct answers. AEO evolved from featured snippet optimisation but now encompasses a broader set of AI platforms.</p>

    <h3>How AEO works</h3>

    <p>An answer engine follows a predictable pattern when it receives a query. It identifies the intent, retrieves candidate sources, extracts the most relevant passage and presents it as a direct answer. AEO targets each step in this process.</p>

    <p>The critical difference between traditional SEO and AEO lies in content structure. Traditional SEO rewards comprehensive, long-form content that keeps users on the page. AEO rewards content that provides a clear, extractable answer within the first few sentences of each section, followed by supporting detail. The answer must be self-contained: a paragraph that can stand alone when extracted from its surrounding context.</p>

    <h3>AEO for Guildford businesses</h3>

    <p>Local AEO is particularly relevant for service-based businesses in Guildford. When a user asks Google's AI Overview "how much does a loft conversion cost in Surrey" or asks ChatGPT "best accountants near Guildford", the AI needs a source that provides a clear, factual answer with local specificity.</p>

    <p>Businesses near the <strong>University of Surrey</strong> campus, along <strong>Guildford High Street</strong> and in the <strong>Tunsgate Quarter</strong> retail area can leverage their physical location as a trust signal. AI platforms increasingly factor geographic relevance into their source selection for local queries.</p>

    <h3>Key AEO tactics</h3>

    <ChecklistFigure title="AEO in practice" items={["Answer in the first sentence","Question-based headings","Visible FAQ with matching schema","Self-contained paragraphs","Specific facts with source and date"]} caption="Each item is a change to pages you already have." />

    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">04 / GEO</p>
    <h2>GEO: generative engine optimisation</h2>

    <p>Generative Engine Optimisation is the newest discipline within AI search optimisation. GEO focuses specifically on how generative AI platforms decide which sources to cite in their synthesised responses. These platforms produce original text rather than retrieving existing text.</p>

    <h3>How LLMs decide what to cite</h3>

    <p>These are my working assumptions from the studies listed in my <a href="/generative-engine-optimisation/">GEO guide</a>, not published rules.</p>

    <p>A generative AI platform does not simply copy text from a source when it produces an answer. It synthesises information from multiple sources and decides which to reference. This decision is influenced by several factors:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Entity salience.</strong> How clearly and consistently your business is defined as an entity across the web. A business with a consistent name, address, and description across its website, Google Business Profile, LinkedIn, and industry directories has higher entity salience than one with fragmented or inconsistent information.</li>
      <li><strong>Source authority.</strong> The AI platform's assessment of your site's trustworthiness, based on factors like domain age, backlink profile, content quality, and consistency with other authoritative sources.</li>
      <li><strong>Content extractability.</strong> How easily the AI can identify and extract relevant information from your pages. Well-structured content with clear headings, short paragraphs, and schema markup is more extractable than dense, unstructured text.</li>
      <li><strong>Topical relevance.</strong> How closely your content matches the specific query. A page that comprehensively covers a narrow topic is more likely to be cited than a page that briefly mentions it among many other topics.</li>
      <li><strong>Corroboration.</strong> AI platforms give more weight to information that multiple independent sources confirm. Your business claims may be supported by third-party reviews, industry publications and directory listings. Citation likelihood then increases.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>GEO in practice</h3>

    <p>Effective GEO requires a methodical approach to entity management. This means keeping your Guildford business's entity information (business name, services, location, credentials) consistent and detailed. Do this across every platform where your business appears: your website, Google Business Profile, Companies House listing, LinkedIn, industry associations and local directories.</p>

    <p>Chambers of commerce, local directories and sector-specific associations all contribute to your entity graph. Each consistent mention reinforces the AI's confidence in citing you as a source.</p>

    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">05 / LLM SEO</p>
    <h2>LLM SEO: how large language models find information</h2>

    <p>LLM SEO addresses the mechanics of how large language models discover, index, and surface information from your website. Understanding these mechanics is essential for effective AI search optimisation.</p>

    <h3>Training data vs retrieval-augmented generation</h3>

    <p>LLMs acquire information through two distinct channels. The first is training data: the massive corpus of web content, books, and other text that the model was trained on. Information in the training data is "baked in" to the model's weights and influences its responses even without real-time web access. The second channel is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). The model searches the web in real time, retrieves relevant pages and incorporates that information into its response.</p>

    <p>Training data influence depends on three factors. These are your site's historical presence in Common Crawl and other web archives, the consistency of your entity information over time and the authority of pages that link to or mention your business. RAG influence depends on factors closer to traditional SEO: current search rankings, page structure, content relevance and technical accessibility.</p>

    <h3>How LLMs process web pages</h3>

    <p>An LLM does not experience a retrieved web page the way a human does. It processes the text content, largely ignoring visual design. This means that information conveyed only through images, JavaScript-rendered content, or complex CSS layouts may be invisible to the LLM. Content must be in the HTML text layer to be retrievable.</p>

    <p>LLMs also have context window limitations. They can only process a certain amount of text at once. Pages that front-load the most important information and use clear hierarchical structure (H1, H2, H3, H4 in proper order) make it easier for the LLM to identify and extract relevant passages.</p>

    <h3>Optimising for LLM retrieval</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Semantic HTML.</strong> Use proper heading hierarchy, lists, tables, and definition lists to structure information.</li>
      <li><strong>Server-side rendering.</strong> Ensure all content is in the initial HTML response, not loaded via JavaScript.</li>
      <li><strong>Concise, factual prose.</strong> LLMs prefer declarative sentences that state facts directly.</li>
      <li><strong>Structured data.</strong> JSON-LD schema provides machine-readable entity and content information that LLMs can parse reliably.</li>
      <li><strong>Internal linking with descriptive anchors.</strong> Helps LLMs understand the relationship between your pages and your topical authority.</li>
    </ul>

