    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">13 / FAQ</p>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Will AI search replace Google?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>No. Google has put AI Overviews into its own results. Some people start research in ChatGPT or Perplexity. You need to be findable in both.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How do I get cited by ChatGPT?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>ChatGPT search fetches live pages through OAI-SearchBot and names the ones it uses. What helped in the studies I list in my GEO guide: pages indexed in normal search, titles that match the question, a clear answer near the top, and consistent business facts across your site and listings. No one can guarantee a citation.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What is llms.txt?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>llms.txt is a plain-text summary of your site for AI tools, placed at your domain root. Google says Search does not use it and it neither helps nor hurts rankings. It is harmless to keep and not a way to get cited.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How long until AI SEO shows results?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>I cannot promise a date. I run a fixed set of prompts monthly from the start. You see citation and description changes as they happen. I count AI referral visits and enquiries separately.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Do I need different content for AI search?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>You do not need entirely separate content. Instead, you need to restructure existing content for extractability. Lead with direct answers, use clear heading hierarchies, implement comprehensive schema markup and ensure your entity information is consistent across the web. The same content serves both traditional and AI search when formatted correctly. The main adjustment is structural rather than substantive: how you present information matters as much as what you say.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What is AEO?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>AEO stands for Answer Engine Optimisation. It is the practice of optimising content to appear as direct answers in AI-powered search tools like Google AI Overviews, ChatGPT, and Perplexity. AEO evolved from featured snippet optimisation but now encompasses a broader set of AI platforms. The core principle is structuring content so that AI systems can extract a clear, self-contained answer from your page and cite it in their response.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What is GEO?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>GEO stands for Generative Engine Optimisation. It is the discipline of optimising content to be selected and cited by generative AI platforms such as ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity. GEO focuses on how large language models decide which sources to reference. This involves entity salience, source authority signals and content structure that aligns with LLM retrieval patterns. AEO targets direct answer extraction. GEO targets the source selection process within generative models.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How does Perplexity find sources?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>Perplexity uses real-time web search combined with retrieval-augmented generation. Perplexity sends search queries to multiple search engines and retrieves the top results. It then uses an LLM to synthesise an answer with inline citations. Every Perplexity answer includes numbered references linking to source pages. Sites that rank well in organic search and have clear, authoritative, well-structured content are most likely to be retrieved and cited by Perplexity.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How do you measure success in AI search optimisation?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>AI search performance is measured through brand monitoring for citation frequency and manual AI query testing across target platforms. Referral traffic from AI platforms such as ChatGPT, Perplexity and Copilot is tracked via custom GA4 channel groupings. Indirect signals, including featured snippet capture rate and branded search volume, also tend to rise alongside effective AI search optimisation.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Is GEO the same thing as SEO?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>GEO and SEO overlap substantially rather than being entirely separate disciplines. Both reward authoritative, well-structured, topically relevant content. Pages that rank well organically tend to be cited more often by AI platforms. The distinction is that SEO optimises for position on a results page. GEO optimises for selection as a cited source within a synthesised AI answer.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

